+def given_for_job(given, reader, stage):
+    """The configuration a stage's Job is served under. PURE; shares nothing.
+
+    W370555. THE ONE ACCOUNT OF THIS RULE, read by the serving composition and
+    by the read-only observation that re-authors the same launch to adopt it.
+    It is a module function rather than a method on either of them because the
+    defect this corrects was two copies of one rule disagreeing:
+    `_SingleWorker._job_execution` composed the Job's own manifest while
+    `_Observation._job_execution` named the installed one, so adoption's
+    exact-byte comparison refused every Job-bound delivery and the projection
+    reported `unreadable (policy/denied)` on every pass while serving was
+    working normally. A reader that cannot reproduce the bytes reports every
+    healthy stage as broken, which is worse than reporting nothing.
+
+    `given` IS NEVER WRITTEN TO. The shared installed document is RETURNED
+    ITSELF when the stage has no own task -- the ordinary case and every Job
+    that predates the per-Job relation -- and a FRESH copy otherwise, so one
+    worker serving two Jobs cannot carry one into the other. Callers compare
+    identity (`is`) to tell the two apart and this keeps that true.
+
+    `None` FROM THE READER IS THE INSTALLED DELIVERY, and three different Jobs
+    give it: one submitted by hand, one created before the relation existed,
+    and one created against a deployment that composes no per-Job task. All
+    three deliver the installed task, which is the behaviour every Job had
+    before W363066.
+
+    A MISSING READER IS ALSO THE INSTALLED DELIVERY, because a composition
+    built without one is asking an installed-delivery question:
+    `test_single_worker`'s harness builds a worker with no store, port, engine
+    or credential provider for exactly that, and an observation over a command
+    that named no Job store has nothing to ask.
+
+    AND THE MANIFEST AND THE TASK TRAVEL TOGETHER. `_task_bytes` proved the
+    installed bytes against the installed manifest's own human contract, so a
+    per-Job task beside the installed manifest is a pair that validator would
+    never have accepted -- `_served_deployment`'s own sentence, and the reason
+    this answers both or neither.
+
+    IT RESOLVES NOTHING ELSE. The policy digest, the limits, the launch
+    contract, the role and the allocation stay exactly as the deployment
+    configured them: this substitutes the input manifest and the task bytes
+    and touches no other member.
+    """
+    if reader is None or stage is None:
+        return given
+    held = reader(job_id=stage.get("job_id"))
+    if held is None:
+        return given
+    from tools import job_task as composer
+
+    return dict(given,
+                input_manifest=composer.manifest_for(given["input_manifest"],
+                                                     held),
+                task_bytes=composer.delivered_bytes(held))
+
+
